


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: drescher
@title: Digital Perspectives on Corelli’s Trio Sonatas
@presenter: drescher
@abstract: 

This presentation introduces a digital research and
teaching platform developed within the seminar “Digital
Methods for the Analysis of Corelli’s Trio Sonatas” at
the University of Music Freiburg during the winter semester
2025/26. The project combines analytical approaches with
digital tools to make structural and stylistic features of
Corelli’s trio sonatas directly accessible within the
musical score.

The platform renders key music-theoretical concepts –
such as cadences, sequences (“Satzmodelle”), and tonal
areas – explorable within an interactive environment.
These features are based on manually created annotations.
Users can search for these phenomena across the entire
corpus and visually highlight them in the score, enabling
an exploratory and practice-oriented approach to analysis.

A central aim of the project is pedagogical. It supports
students working with Baroque music and historical
compositional techniques by providing a framework for
targeted inquiry. Students can further develop their own
compositions by directly comparing specific musical
phenomena with corresponding passages in Corelli’s works.
In addition, broader corpus-wide statistical analyses allow
these observations to be contextualized and verified. For
example, recurring patterns such as descending and
ascending syncopation chains can be examined in their
contrapuntal and harmonic context. This makes it possible
to understand how such patterns work within Corelli’s
compositional language and how they contribute to a larger
formal structure.

Overall, the interactive platform demonstrates how digital
tools can extend traditional music theory by making
analytical structures visible and usable within the
composition itself, opening further perspectives for
theoretical and musicological research on certain Baroque
repertoire.

@@END: PRESENTATION



